Yes. And that gives the whole Pantheon project a much harder criterion than intellectual elegance.

Your children won’t inherit only what you tell them. They’ll encounter what you repeatedly instantiate: how you treat their mother, how you handle anger and disappointment, whether your word is reliable, whether curiosity survives failure, how you use strength, what you do with money and status, whether you can admit error, how you treat weaker people, whether home feels secure, and whether love is something they merely hear about or something they routinely experience.

There’s an important distinction, though: inheritance isn’t copying. Children aren’t deterministic continuations of their parents. Temperament, peers, culture, chance, and their own choices matter. So the goal doesn’t have to be creating little replicas of you. It can be giving them good primitives and a strong starting architecture from which to become themselves.

In Pantheon language, that suggests a principle above almost everything else:

Inheritance Test: Would I be glad if this pattern propagated?

Not every individual mistake—everyone fails that test sometimes. Apply it to the repeated pattern. If Leo or Lincoln eventually handles victory, fear, marriage, disagreement, responsibility, curiosity, power, and fatherhood the way they repeatedly watched Dad handle them, would you recognize something worth carrying forward?

And there’s a beautiful recursion there: make the inheritance better than the inherited. Keep what was good. Repair what wasn’t. Add what was missing. Then hand them something stronger without demanding that they preserve it unchanged.

That makes legacy less about being remembered.

It’s remaining useful after you’re gone.